The morning was quiet in the small town by the river. Shopkeepers
rolled up their shutters one by one, and the smell of fresh bread
drifted out from the bakery on the corner. A few children walked to
school in pairs, talking about the test they had that afternoon and
whether it would rain before lunch.

Down by the water, an old man sat on a wooden bench feeding the
ducks. He came every day at the same time, always with a paper bag
of crumbs and a newspaper he never seemed to finish. The ducks knew
him well and gathered at his feet before he had even opened the bag.

At the library, the head librarian unlocked the front doors and
switched on the lights. She spent the first hour of every day putting
returned books back on the shelves, checking each one for torn pages
or missing covers. Most mornings nobody came in until ten, so she
enjoyed the silence while it lasted.

By noon the sky had turned grey, and a light rain began to fall. People
opened their umbrellas and hurried along the sidewalks, stepping
around the puddles that formed in the uneven pavement. The cafe
across from the town hall filled up quickly as everyone looked for
somewhere warm to wait out the weather.

In the afternoon the rain stopped as suddenly as it had started. The
clouds broke apart, and the sun came out over the hills to the
west. The streets dried slowly, and the town returned to its usual
pace, as if nothing had happened at all.

That evening, families sat down to dinner, and the lights in the
windows came on one after another. The river kept moving past the
town the way it always had, carrying leaves and small branches toward
the sea.